  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Good answer to the carrier-versus-protocol boundary, weak answer to chunk-feed semantics. The envelope can ride unchanged over many substrates and signs canonical [pcid,payload] bytes, so integrity is substrate-independent. But replication semantics, chunk ordering, and carrier-specific recovery all live outside this draft, so the scenario is only partially covered.",
    "strengths": [
      "Same canonical DAG-CBOR envelope can cross multiple carriers unchanged.",
      "Signature binds pCID plus payload, preserving transport-independent integrity and local verification.",
      "pCID dispatch with explicit unsupported/unverified handling fits sparse-knowledge auditability."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "No feed or chunk-replication protocol is defined; sequencing, retry, and convergence are delegated elsewhere.",
      "Relay or hop-local evidence may live in wrapper, payload, or signature slots, leaving interop choices open.",
      "The spec is still a draft and the specimen pCID is not yet minted."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Implementers may reintroduce carrier-specific semantics in wrappers and lose substrate independence.",
      "Best-effort inspection of unknown pCID data could be mistaken for verified interpretation.",
      "Mandatory signatures may add overhead or new failure points on very small or legacy carriers."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "What payload protocol defines chunk IDs, sequencing, and replication state across carriers?",
      "What should peers do when sig_pcid is unknown but storage or forwarding is still desired?",
      "For MTU-limited carriers, should large chunks travel inline or by content-addressed reference inside payload?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
